  "response": "The failed unit was a 230/69 kV oil-immersed power transformer at the utility's substation. Two incompatible accounts of the event were placed on the record by the parties. The utility attributed the failure to a defect in the high-voltage winding insulation, whereas the manufacturer attributed the failure to a switching transient produced by capacitor bank operation on the adjacent feeder. Resolving a dispute of this kind is a matter of ordering the physical evidence, since the diagnostic signatures of an internally initiated fault and of an externally imposed overvoltage differ in kind. Engineers therefore begin not with the parties' assertions but with the records that the apparatus and its protection scheme generated independently of either of them.\n\nDissolved gas analysis measures fault gases dissolved in transformer insulating oil. Since measuring those dissolved fault gases is the whole of what the analysis does, it cannot also identify the exact calendar date on which an internal fault initiated. Applied to oil drawn from the failed unit, that measurement is what produced the central laboratory finding of the investigation. The dissolved gas analysis showed 480 ppm of acetylene in the oil sample, a reading that confirms the rule that elevated acetylene concentrations in insulating oil indicate high-energy arcing.\n\nBy contrast, elevated carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide levels in the oil indicate degradation of cellulose paper insulation, a signature that implicates the solid rather than the liquid portion of the insulation system. Digital fault recorders at the substation captured voltage and current waveforms during the event. Such records permit the sequence of voltage collapse and current rise to be reconstructed at sub-cycle resolution, which is the only way to establish whether the disturbance on the system preceded the collapse of dielectric strength in the apparatus or followed it. The surge arrester discharge counter at the transformer terminals registered the number of surge operations. Surge arresters eliminate all transient overvoltages at transformer terminals.\n\nAfter the waveform records had been secured, untanking and internal inspection located the failure site within the winding, precisely the region that the elevated carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide readings had indicated. The geometry of the damage then becomes decisive: a puncture concentrated at the line-end turns, where impulse stress accumulates, carries a different implication from a failure distributed through the body of the coil. Taken together, the oil chemistry, the recorded waveforms, the surge history at the terminals and the physical condition of the conductor and paper permit the investigator to determine which of the two competing accounts the hardware itself will support, and to discard the other.\n\nOne further matter conditions the commercial consequences of whatever technical finding is reached. Either the transformer was within its manufacturer warranty period on the date of failure, or the manufacturer's warranty had expired before the date of failure; one of those two situations must be the case, and they cannot both obtain.",
